      "type": "title",
      "text": "None of This Belongs Here"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "They landed in the east and developed towns, cities, and states. The east was planned and established organically, in harmony with the topography and the surrounding landscape. The landscape and climate were largely similar to what had existed in the Old World.\n\nExpeditions westward expanded into a territory wholly unlike what was known before. It revealed a scale that almost defied comprehension. Faced with this vast and unfamiliar expanse, there was no way to settle it organically; the primary way to bring order to this wilderness was a geometric grid of one-mile squares.\n\nLacking steady water and shelter, the High Plains are often considered a quiet liminal space stretched between two coasts. The lush variety of eastern forests and oceans gives way to an environment that is very much the opposite: relentless winds, dry earth, and abundant, unfiltered sun. It is a demanding climate that steadily tests everything existing upon it, gradually wearing away whatever has been built or grown and returning the landscape to its natural state.\n\nThis grid is manufactured and inorganic, set against the ancient rhythm of the land. Indigenous peoples inhabited this place but built no permanent structures here - partly because the land provided no materials for permanence, and partly because little ultimately subsists against the elements.\n\nUltimately, none of this belongs here."
    },
